  "content": {
    "id": "p35-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Decide whether the inverse of $y=\\frac{2^x-2^{-x}}{2}$ is a function.\n\n(You will need to differentiate it to decide whether it is strictly increasing or decreasing).",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes. $f'(x)=\\frac{\\ln 2\\,(2^x+2^{-x})}{2}>0$ for all real $x$, so $f$ is strictly increasing and its inverse is a function.",
      "worked": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p35-q14-answer",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{2^x\\ln 2+2^{-x}\\ln 2}{2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{\\ln 2\\,(2^x+2^{-x})}{2}.\\end{align*}",
                "display": true
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p35-q14-answer-reason",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Since ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\ln 2>0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " and both exponential terms are positive, ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "f'(x)>0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " for every real ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "x",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ". Thus the function is strictly increasing throughout its domain and is one-to-one. Its inverse is therefore a function.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
